# Rule 1 of the product direction (issue #3): never fight macOS.
# Story: somebody starts Arbor on a Mac that is set as macOS comes. macOS's own tiling wants the same screen edges. Arbor knows, and
# tells whoever asks, before it has arranged anything. A plain drag is macOS's own, so nothing anybody does unasked runs into it:
# it is something to know, and no problem.
# This is what Arbor knows and says. That it stands aside where macOS acts on a drag is in `MacTilingTests`; the fix in one
# step is `fix-setup`.
quiet
macos defaults
open Safari
open Notes
doctor
expect ok
expect replied macOS’s own tiling is on, and gets a window dragged to a screen edge first, with ⌘ held too
expect replied Drag windows to left or right edge of screen to tile
expect replied Drag windows to menu bar to fill screen
expect replied Hold ⌥ key while dragging windows to tile
expect replied Click wallpaper to reveal desktop
# Asking moved nothing, and nothing is arranged.
expect still
expect count docked 0
# Where a plain drag docks, the two want the same drags. `doctor` fails while there is something to put right, so that a script
# notices too.
set dragDocks always
doctor
expect fail
expect replied macOS reacts to the same clicks and drags as Arbor
expect replied Drag windows to left or right edge of screen to tile
expect still
# With the switches off there is nothing left to say.
macos tidy
doctor
expect ok
expect replied none in the way
expect still
